# Line endings are a licence-gate concern in this repository, not a style preference.
#
# `bun run licenses --check` compares the COMMITTED attribution byte for byte against what
# the generator would write from what is actually bundled, and the generator always writes
# LF. Git-for-Windows sets `core.autocrlf=true` at SYSTEM level — this project has already
# found that brigadier only empties the GLOBAL config — so without this file a Windows
# checkout hands the checker CRLF copies of `THIRD-PARTY.md` and `src/generated/licenses.ts`
# and it calls them stale. That is what killed the first ever Windows CI run, in the FIRST
# command of the build step, before `scripts/build.ts` ran at all (commit 7ff6431).
#
# MEASURED on 2026-08-20, on darwin, by cloning this repository with `-c core.autocrlf=true`
# and running `bun run scripts/licenses.ts --check` in the clone: both surfaces reported
# STALE, and both were byte-identical to the regenerated ones once CR bytes were removed —
# the attribution was never wrong. The same checkout also hands the GENERATOR CRLF copies of
# `LICENSE` and `vendor/*`, whose bytes are reprinted verbatim into the attribution and into
# the compiled binary: regenerating there produced a `src/generated/licenses.ts` carrying
# 1,272 `\r` escapes inside the shipped licence strings. That is the more serious half — the
# artifact would have differed by platform, and the artifact is what the recipient receives.
#
# This file changes checkout behaviour for EVERYONE, which is the point: the committed bytes
# are the artifact, so every platform must be given the committed bytes. Existing clones do
# not update themselves — a clone made before this file may hold CRLF working copies until
# `git add --renormalize .` or a fresh checkout. CI clones fresh, so CI is covered.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The attribution surfaces and every committed file whose bytes they reprint. Declared
# explicitly rather than left to `text=auto`'s content sniffing, because these are the exact
# bytes `bun run licenses --check` compares and `bun run license-gate` looks for in the binary.
LICENSE                                     text eol=lf
THIRD-PARTY.md                              text eol=lf
RELINKING.md                                text eol=lf
src/generated/licenses.ts                   text eol=lf
vendor/pins.json                            text eol=lf
vendor/lgpl-census.json                     text eol=lf
vendor/bun-LICENSE.md                       text eol=lf
vendor/javascriptcore-webkit-COPYING.LIB    text eol=lf
vendor/tinycc-COPYING                       text eol=lf

# `scripts/license-gate.ts` embeds a literal NUL byte on purpose (its own header records it,
# and plain `grep` reports zero matches on it rather than an error). `text=auto` sniffs that
# as binary and would leave it out of normalisation entirely, so a Windows editor could commit
# CRLF into it unnoticed; declaring it text keeps its endings pinned. It holds no CR bytes
# today, so this is a no-op on the current blob.
scripts/license-gate.ts                     text eol=lf

# Compressed grammars: bytes that must survive verbatim, and must never be diffed or merged.
vendor/grammars/*.gz                        binary
